.TH ntpdate 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
ntpdate \- set the clock from a time server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ntpdate
.I server
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ntpdate
asks
.I server
for the time, sets the kernel clock to it, and prints the result.
.PP
It sets the kernel clock only.  Follow it with
.B setdate \-w
to write the time to the DS3231, so that it survives a power cut.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# ntpdate time.cloudflare.com
# setdate -w
.fi
.SH NOTES
Do this before anything involving TLS.  A certificate is only valid
between two dates, so a machine that thinks it is 1970 rejects every
certificate on the internet \- and the error will not mention the clock.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR setdate (1),
.BR date (1),
.BR tlsca (1),
.BR wifi (1)
